<excerpt># v1.9.0 `@cfworker/json-schema/dist/validator.js` - Error when importing - State: closed - Author: pbeck6 - Created: 2021-11-13T05:34:52Z - Updated: 2021-11-20T14:31:02Z - Repository: cfworker/cfworker - Number: `#156` ## Labels - question --- The following code in validator.js: ``` export class Validator { schema; draft; shortCircuit; lookup; constructor(schema, draft = &`#39`;2019-09&`#39`;, shortCircuit = true) { this.schema = schema; this.draft = draft; this.shortCircuit = shortCircuit; this.lookup = dereference(schema); // rest of code } ``` causes a loader error when attempting to `import { Validator } from &`#39`;`@cfworker/json-schema`&`#39`;`. It appears JS does not understand `schema, draft, shortCircuit, lookup` public instance fields appearing before constructor. Issue is fixed after removing. Working validator.js code: ``` export class Validator { constructor(schema, draft = &`#39`;2019-09&`#39`;, shortCircuit = true) { this.schema = schema; this.draft = draft; this.shortCircuit = shortCircuit; this.lookup = dereference(schema); // rest of code } ``` ## Timeline **jdanyow** commented on 2021-11-14T04:12:25Z: &gt; Those are public instance fields. Where are you seeing the error? The 1.9.0 code runs in the browser/workers without any compilation. &gt; &gt; ```html &gt; &gt; &gt; &gt; &gt; GistRun &gt; &gt; import { Validator } from &`#39`;https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@cfworker/json-schema@1.9.0/dist/index.js&`#39`;; &gt; const validator = new Validator({ type: &`#39`;number&`#39`; }); &gt; let result = validator.validate(1); &gt; document.body.insertAdjacentHTML(&`#39`;beforeend&`#39`;, ` ${JSON.stringify(result, null, 2)} `); &gt; result = validator.validate(&`#39`;hello&`#39`;); &gt; document.body.insertAdjacentHTML(&`#39`;beforeend&`#39`;, ` ${JSON.stringify(result, null, 2)} `); &gt; &gt; &gt; &gt; Hello world! &gt; &gt; &gt; ``` &gt; https://gist.run/?id=a4f6c9b5854d10bcfe9c39f7345bcd10 - jdanyow added label &quot;question&quot; **pbeck6** commented on 2021-11-14T04:36:55Z: &gt; The error appeared while attempting to publish a Worker via Wrangler from a local machine. I had installed `@cfworker/json-schema` locally via npm (v6.14.14) &gt; &gt; edit: Also would like to say thanks to everyone who contributed to these packages. I was struggling with Ajv for a bit until I found this. &gt; &gt; ``` &gt; WebPack Error: You may need an appropriate loader to handle this file type, currently no loaders are configured to process this file. &gt; | import { dereference } from &`#39`;./dereference.js&`#39`;; &gt; | import { validate } from &`#39`;./validate.js&`#39`;; &gt; | export class Validator { &gt; &gt; schema; &gt; ``` - pbeck6 closed **apeiniger** commented on 2021-11-18T15:35:51Z: &gt; `@stoneroll6` Could you share how you solved this issue? Having the same problem. I&`#39`;m using `@cfworker/json-schema` in my create react app project and since 1.9.0 it&`#39`;s throwing this error when building. - pbeck6 mentioned - pbeck6 subscribed **pbeck6** commented on 2021-11-19T01:26:26Z: &gt; `@apeiniger` Yes, please see original post above for details. If the validator.js file is throwing the error when building, you can try removing the public instance fields. &gt; &gt; Not sure if this will cause errors down the line, but schema validation was working after doing so. - apeiniger mentioned - apeiniger subscribed - Referenced by PR `#161`: Target ES2021 **jdanyow** commented on 2021-11-19T23:31:13Z: &gt; `@apeiniger` `@stoneroll6` try 1.10.1. I&`#39`;ve updated the build to target ES2021 rather than ESNEXT which has the effect of removing the instance fields in the output. This should give your build tooling some time to catch up. Please do let me know if this resolves the issue. - apeiniger mentioned - apeiniger subscribed - pbeck6 mentioned - pbeck6 subscribed **pbeck6** commented on 2021-11-20T14:31:02Z: &gt; `@jdanyow` Updated to 1.10.1, and it works like a charm. Thank you! - jdanyow mentioned - jdanyow subscribed</excerpt>
